Turn embedded ancillary windows along with the playfield.

The cabinet view stands the table up on a landscape panel mounted upright by
turning the 3D scene 270 degrees inside a framebuffer that stays landscape
(ViewSetup::GetRotation). Only the scene is turned: ancillary windows are
composited in screen space afterwards, so an embedded score panel keeps reading
sideways to the player, at a right angle to the table it belongs to.

It goes unnoticed upstream because the combination never occurs there: a cab
gives its DMD a screen of its own (a floating window, which rightly keeps the
orientation of the screen it sits on), and desktop players do not rotate
anything. It only shows up when the whole cabinet has to fit on a single
standing screen, where the score panel must be embedded.

All ancillary drawing (DrawImage, DrawMatrixDisplay, DrawSegmentDisplay) submits
its quads in the unit square, and a single ortho matrix built here maps them onto
the region, so composing the rotation into that matrix turns every renderer at
once. The renderers are then handed the size they draw into rather than the one
the region occupies on screen, which keeps their own fitting logic untouched: the
ScoreView plugin still sees the 4:1 panel its layouts are cut for, picks the same
layout and fits it the same way.

Embedded windows only: a floating window matches its own screen and must not
follow the playfield.

The region itself is not turned, only its content: a quarter turn wants a portrait
region, which is for the caller to set.

Signed-off-by: Adrien Beudin <beudbeud@gmail.com>

